'''Cases Justes, S.A.''' ({{lang|ca|Cases Justes}}; "Fair Homes") is a Balboan real estate development and property management company and the country's largest suburban homebuilder and residential landlord. The company designs, builds, and sells single-family detached homes and townhouses in suburban communities across the isthmus, and retains a significant portfolio of rental properties. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker CJ.

Founded in 1978 by [[Pere Comas i Riera]], a builder from the [[Estret Province|Estret]] corridor, the company targeted a segment of the post-reunification housing market that was underserved by the major urban developers: middle-class families seeking detached homes with gardens in suburban settings rather than the mid-rise apartments that dominated the Balboan urban landscape. The company's name — "Cases Justes," meaning "Fair Homes" — was intended to signal a commitment to affordable, quality construction for the Balboan middle class.

Cases Justes is distinctive among Balboan real estate companies for its dual business model, combining for-sale suburban development with a rental portfolio of approximately 4,800 homes — the largest suburban rental portfolio in the country. The rental portfolio provides a recurring revenue stream that partially offsets the cyclicality of the development business.

== History ==

=== Founding and early years (1978–1990) ===

[[Pere Comas i Riera]] began his career as a carpenter in the Estret corridor and moved into small-scale homebuilding in the early 1970s, constructing a handful of single-family homes each year. The post-reunification economic expansion of the 1960s and 1970s had produced a growing middle class — beneficiaries of the land reform, the cooperative movement, and industrial development — many of whom sought detached homes with gardens in the suburbs. The major developers, including [[Construccions de Sant Cristòfor|CSC]], were focused on mid-rise urban apartments, and the suburban market was served only by small-scale builders.

Comas incorporated Cases Justes in 1978 and developed his first suburban community — a 40-home project on the outskirts of the Estret corridor, with detached single-family homes on individual lots. The concept was initially met with scepticism from the established real estate sector, which viewed suburban detached housing as a departure from the Balboan urban tradition of apartment living, but the homes sold rapidly to middle-class families.

The company's early growth was concentrated in the Estret corridor, where rapid population growth and migration from the interior created sustained demand for suburban housing. Cases Justes built a series of communities ranging from 40 to 100 homes, establishing a reputation for quality construction and competitive pricing.

=== Expansion and the rental portfolio (1990–2000) ===

In the 1990s, Cases Justes expanded beyond the Estret corridor into the suburban markets of the interior cities, opening a regional office in [[Sant Bart]] in 1990. The company also began building its rental portfolio during this period, retaining a portion of the homes in each development as rental properties rather than selling them. The strategy was designed to address the underserved suburban rental market — most rental housing in Balboa consisted of urban apartments — and to provide a recurring revenue stream that would stabilise the company through the real estate cycle.

The rental strategy was controversial within the company's board, which was concerned that retaining properties would tie up capital that could be deployed into new development. The strategy was vindicated during the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] and the [[1994 Balboa spot recession]], when the development business was affected by the collapse of the housing market but the rental portfolio continued to generate income.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1998, with the Comas family retaining a 35 per cent stake. The listing proceeds were used to expand both the development pipeline and the rental portfolio.

=== Modern era (2000–present) ===

During the 2000s and 2010s, Cases Justes developed larger communities — ranging from 200 to 500 homes — in the Estret corridor and the interior cities, and expanded its rental portfolio to approximately 4,800 homes. The [[Adonian pandemic]] of 2020 produced a surge in demand for suburban housing, as urban residents sought larger homes with gardens and outdoor space for remote work. The company's development pipeline accelerated to meet the demand, and the value of the rental portfolio increased as suburban rental homes became more desirable.

By 2026, Cases Justes had built over 18,000 homes across 85 communities and employed approximately 5,400 people, making it the largest suburban homebuilder in Balboa.

=== Rental standards inquisition (2019) ===

In 2019, Cases Justes was the subject of a formal investigation by the [[Balboan Housing Standards Authority]] (AHHE) into the condition of its rental properties in lower-income communities. The investigation was triggered by tenant complaints and media reports documenting substandard living conditions in several Cases Justes rental developments, particularly in [[Enllaç]], [[Cumara]], and in [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]], the lowest-income district of the [[Sant Cristòfor]] metropolitan area.

The AHHE investigation documented over 600 individual housing standard violations across 12 developments and concluded that the company's property management division had systematically allocated maintenance resources in favour of higher-rent properties, resulting in delayed repairs for plumbing and electrical faults, persistent damp and mould, broken communal facilities, and inadequate waste management at lower-income developments.

The case attracted significant public attention and was widely characterised in the Balboan media as a "slumlord" scandal. The contrast between the company's "Cases Justes" — "Fair Homes" — branding and the conditions documented in the investigation was the subject of extensive media commentary. The company's share price declined by 18 per cent in the weeks following the publication of the AHHE report.

Cases Justes agreed to a regulatory settlement with the AHHE, which included a $12 million fine, a $45 million remediation programme for repairs and upgrades across the affected developments, and ongoing compliance monitoring. The Comas family issued a public apology acknowledging that the company had failed to uphold the standards implied by its name. The case prompted the Balboan parliament to strengthen the housing standards enforcement framework for large-scale residential landlords, and AHHE compliance monitoring continued through 2026.

=== Pandemic rental assistance (2020) ===

During the [[Adonian pandemic]] in 2020, the [[Central Bank of Balboa]] provided Cases Justes with a $180 million low-interest loan to fund a rental assistance programme intended to prevent evictions in the company's lower-income rental communities, where tenants had lost income due to pandemic lockdowns. The programme provided rent subsidies and eviction moratoriums for approximately 1,200 households across the affected developments, including those in [[Enllaç]], [[Cumara]], and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]].

The bailout was controversial. Tenant advocacy groups and opposition politicians argued that the assistance should have been distributed directly to citizens as cash grants rather than routed through their landlord, and that the government's decision to channel the funds through Cases Justes prioritised the company's financial stability over the autonomy and dignity of tenants. The arrangement became a rallying point for housing rights advocates, who argued that pandemic relief should have been administered by the state's social services apparatus rather than by a private landlord that had been fined for housing standards violations the previous year. The government defended the arrangement as the fastest mechanism for delivering relief, citing Cases Justes's existing property management infrastructure across the affected communities. The loan was repaid in full by 2024.

== Operations ==

Cases Justes operates across two principal business lines:

* '''Suburban development''': The company's core business, comprising the design, construction, and sale of single-family detached homes and townhouses in suburban communities across the Estret corridor and the interior cities. Developments range from 40-home communities in smaller towns to 500-home communities in the Estret corridor, each incorporating community amenities including parks, playgrounds, and community centres. The company has built over 18,000 homes across 85 communities since its founding.
* '''Rental property management''': The company manages a portfolio of approximately 4,800 rental homes — primarily single-family homes and townhouses retained from its developments — providing a recurring revenue stream. The portfolio is concentrated in the Estret corridor (60 per cent) and the interior cities (40 per cent). The property management division handles maintenance, leasing, and tenant services.

The company's headquarters are at 180 Carrer Frederic, in the Districte Financer of Sant Cristòfor, and it maintains regional offices in [[Sant Bart]] ([[Jala Province|Jala]]), [[Sant Llàtzer]] ([[Migjorn Province|Migjorn]]), and [[Portblanc]] ([[Tramuntana Province|Tramuntana]]).

